  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $7$, respectively, what is the degree of $P-Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{7}.$ No ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $7.$",
